{"name":"San Gabriel Blue","hex":"#2f6679","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"san","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Sanskrit."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An Ancient Greek letter representing /s/ (uppercase Ϻ, lowercase ϻ), originally used in some dialects instead of sigma."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/san"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gabriel","blocks":[{"text":"In Judaism, Christianity, Islam, and other Abrahamic religions, Gabriel is an archangel with the power to announce God's will to humankind as the messenger of God. He is mentioned in the Hebrew Bible, the New Testament and the Quran. In the Book of Daniel, Gabriel appears to the prophet Daniel to explain his visions. Gabriel also appears in the Jewish apocryphal First Book of Enoch and other ancient Hebrew writings incompletely preserved or wholly lost in Hebrew. Alongside the archangel Michael, Gabriel is described as the guardian angel of the Israelites, defending them against the angels of the other peoples."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Ghent_Altarpiece_-_Angel_of_the_Annunciation.jpg/330px-Ghent_Altarpiece_-_Angel_of_the_Annunciation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel","wikidata":"Q81989"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/The_Archangel_Gabriel_appears_to_the_Prophet_Daniel.png/500px-The_Archangel_Gabriel_appears_to_the_Prophet_Daniel.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/The_Archangel_Gabriel_appears_to_the_Prophet_Daniel.png/500px-The_Archangel_Gabriel_appears_to_the_Prophet_Daniel.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Archangel Gabriel appears to the Prophet Daniel, attributed to Francesco Solimena (1657–1747).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Archangel_Gabriel_appears_to_the_Prophet_Daniel.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Alexandr_Ivanov_010.jpg/500px-Alexandr_Ivanov_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Alexandr_Ivanov_010.jpg/500px-Alexandr_Ivanov_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gabriel announcing the birth of John the Baptist to Zechariah, by Alexander Andreyevich Ivanov, 1824","link":"h